31
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:12,480
So what I want to start with is Mackay Chapter 2, what he calls the balance sheet.

32
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:17,840
And it's an important set of concepts, simply so we can think about the energy system.

33
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:24,720
Very simply, what Mackay is trying to do in his book is contrast these red and green bars.

34
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:30,280
Energy consumption is on the left in red, and energy production is on the right in green.

35
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:35,240
And so when Mackay titles his book, Sustainable Energy Without the Hot Air, what we're trying

36
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:41,480
to do is replace our current level of consumption with entirely renewable resources, and that's

37
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:44,560
how we're going to achieve a sustainable energy system.

38
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:50,680
And so a sustainable energy system would be if our total conceivable sustainable production

39
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:56,000
sources on the right are greater than our total consumption, and an unsustainable energy

40
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,360
system would be where our total consumption of energy is larger than our total conceivable

41
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:02,360
sustainable production.

42
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:08,520
And of course, these two bars will change over time as our expectations around consumption

43
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:14,160
change and our technologies and our institutions and our markets for sustainable production

44
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:15,160
change.

45
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:16,160
But that's the challenge.

46
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:20,640
The challenge is to try to put these two in balance and at least have total sustainable

47
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:26,720
production that is enough to meet all of our societal needs or our global societal needs

48
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:28,800
for energy consumption.

49
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:33,680
And so what that might look like is the key forms of consumption on the left-hand stack

50
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:35,240
in red.

51
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:40,520
That is the energy we need for transportation, including cars, planes, and freight.

52
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:44,920
It's the energy we need to heat and cool the spaces that we live in that can wildly differ

53
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:46,600
across countries.

54
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:50,440
It's a basic need for lighting so we can see at night.

55
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:56,280
It's a growing need for energy for information systems and our other gadgets.

56
00:03:56,280 --> 00:04:03,520
The fifth point, food, a tremendous amount of energy goes into growing and transporting

57
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:07,640
and keeping our food cold and finally manufacturing.

58
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:12,560
For all the technologies or all the gadgets we need to do transportation or heating and

59
00:04:12,560 --> 00:04:16,840
cooling like information systems and food, we have to make these things in the first

60
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:20,200
place and that requires a tremendous amount of energy.

61
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:24,800
On the right-hand side, our sustainable production stack has a number of categories.

62
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:29,640
The good thing is, and I'll try to emphasize this in the class repeatedly, is that we have

63
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:34,080
many sustainable technologies that can produce energy.

64
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:35,960
We have wind and solar.

65
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:40,720
A solar energy can be harnessed in the form of fortable taillight or solar cells that

66
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:42,560
produce electricity.

67
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:48,000
We can harness solar energy through thermal energy and we can also harness solar energy

68
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,480
through biomass by harvesting the potential of the earth to engage in photosynthesis,

69
00:04:53,480 --> 00:05:00,160
the crops that we've been can use for other things, including burning in engines.

70
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:05,880
Hydroelectric power is producing electricity directly from or mechanical storage directly

71
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:07,680
from hydropower.

72
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:15,160
A wave energy and tide energy are two sources of energy that comes from the earth on a regular

73
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:16,160
basis.

74
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:18,960
A geothermal energy also comes from the earth.

75
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:23,920
The heat inside the earth can be harnessed to produce energy, and finally, nuclear.

76
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:28,200
Mackay puts it with a question mark because it's not clear whether or not nuclear energy

77
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:30,480
or nuclear power counts are sustainable.

78
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:32,480
It is low-carbon.

79
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:39,200
As I said in the earlier class, we have questions about how do we handle the fuel for nuclear

80
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:43,040
power plants, how do we handle the waste for nuclear power plants, and whether or not that

81
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:47,280
will be fully sustainable in the long run.

82
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:51,760
I want to cover two physical concepts from Mackay chapter two that I think are basically

83
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:57,280
going to be the most important physical concepts you need to understand this class.

84
00:05:57,280 --> 00:05:59,960
The first one, quite simply, is energy.

85
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:04,160
It's the quantitative property of doing work.

86
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:10,800
Energy can be neither destroyed nor created, one of our fundamental laws of thermodynamics.

87
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:17,200
Energy can be transformed into light, heat, or mass, hence the famous equation by Einstein

88
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:20,320
equals mc squared.

89
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:23,560
Energy equals mass times the speed of light squared.

90
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:27,200
This is a fundamental finding out of relativity.

91
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:31,840
Of course, energy can take many different forms, and we use it in many different forms

92
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:33,520
in our energy system.

93
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:38,520
Energy can be kinetic, it can be chemical, we can have potential energy, we can have

94
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:43,080
mechanical energy, let's say stored in the elastic properties and materials, and of course,

95
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:47,920
biological energy, which is fundamentally chemical energy, but we have different modes

96
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:54,160
of perhaps using that chemical energy in our muscles, in animals, in ecosystems.

97
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:57,800
We're going to measure energy in terms of units of kilowatt hours.

98
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:02,640
You'll see energy referred to in terms of British thermal units, BTUs, something only

99
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:09,800
used in the US and America, the United States, of therms, joules, calories, are all units

100
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:10,800
for energy.

101
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:13,000
Energy can be converted to kilowatt hours.

102
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,800
A specific category I just want to highlight below is fossil fuels are sometimes measured

103
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:24,160
in barrels of oil, short tons of coal, cubic feet of natural gas, that's because that's

104
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:29,680
how we measure the things we trade in commodity markets for commoditized fossil fuels.

105
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:34,400
But again, the amount of energy in a barrel of oil and a short ton of coal and cubic

106
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:39,120
foot of natural gas can be converted to kilowatt hours.

107
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:43,960
So just to give you a few examples, example one, you may have had the frustrating experience

108
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:50,000
of having gone to the gym, foolishly counting your calories, realizing you worked out for

109
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:54,560
an hour on a running treadmill as only equivalent to three Oreos.

110
00:07:54,560 --> 00:08:00,280
You can also look at example two, the average American household uses per year about 11,000

111
00:08:00,280 --> 00:08:08,080
kilowatt hours in electricity, each person uses 300 million BTUs total per year, which

112
00:08:08,080 --> 00:08:15,920
is approximately 2.3 gallons of oil, 7.89 pounds of coal and 252 cubic feet of natural

113
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:17,560
gas per day.

114
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:21,920
Now it's just worth pointing out that the first example in terms of the American household

115
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:25,640
is in kilowatt hours, but only counts for electricity.

116
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:27,840
That's for the whole household per year.

117
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:34,200
The second sentence measures each person on a per capita basis, but then breaks the millions

118
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:39,360
of BTUs into different units because of the different sources of energy.

119
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:44,480
And this is to say that when we do these comparisons, we want to be careful about the units we use,

120
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:49,840
the averaging over time, and the sources of energy because the original source of energy

121
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:52,440
may not actually be how much energy we use in the end.

122
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:56,960
And we'll talk about that more in a few slides.

123
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:01,800
Another key fiscal concept we have to talk about or will be very useful is power.

124
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:05,440
And that's the quantitative rate of doing work.

125
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:09,800
That is, power is energy per time.

126
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:11,040
It's a rate.

127
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:16,680
The units we use of that are watts or equal to joules per second.

128
00:09:16,680 --> 00:09:23,120
Other forms of power that you might be familiar with are urges, amperes in terms of electrical

129
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:30,400
current, horsepower in terms of the engine in your car or other motors, and lumens are

130
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:38,440
the amount of energy cast off per second from an LED light bulb, but measured over a sphere

131
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:39,440
around a light bulb.

132
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:41,920
So it's actually weighted by the area around the light bulb.

133
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:48,680
Or what degree of area is putting off how much energy.

134
00:09:48,680 --> 00:09:53,680
So to just kind of use the same example as the previous slide, you could say, I worked

135
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:58,680
out for an hour and I only worked off the equivalent of three Oreos, but maybe sounds

136
00:09:58,680 --> 00:10:03,880
perhaps more impressive if you say my workout maintained a steady power output of three

137
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:07,400
Oreos per hour.

138
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:12,800
Another key concept we have to cover that links energy and climate is emissions intensity.

139
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:18,360
And emissions are measured in terms of greenhouse gases, usually measured in metric ton carbon

140
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:26,440
dioxide equivalent, which is abbreviated MTCDE or MTCO2E and so on.

141
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:31,480
And the reason why we have to measure it in terms of metric ton carbon dioxide equivalent

142
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:35,880
is that there's a lot of different greenhouse gases and some of them, for example, methane

143
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:40,840
CH4 has much more global warming potential in carbon dioxide.

144
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:47,840
So a single ton of methane has about 84 times the global warming potential of a ton of carbon

145
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:54,280
dioxide in early in this life that declines over time to about 20 metric tons of carbon

146
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:55,720
dioxide equivalent.

147
00:10:55,720 --> 00:11:00,800
So this is just to say that if we have different greenhouse gases, we measure them or make

148
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:06,680
them all equivalent to a metric ton of carbon dioxide equivalent during their lifetime.

149
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:11,840
We can also measure emissions intensity by the type of gas, as the example I just gave

150
00:11:11,840 --> 00:11:17,920
you in terms of methane, also nitrous oxide and fluoride hexanes, I think, are another

151
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:19,080
greenhouse gas.

152
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:22,400
You can measure emissions intensity per unit of energy.

153
00:11:22,400 --> 00:11:26,880
This much greenhouse gas is created in terms of this much energy.

154
00:11:26,880 --> 00:11:31,960
You can measure greenhouse gases per activity, per dollar GDP or by region.

155
00:11:31,960 --> 00:11:33,920
People use lots of different intensities.

156
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:37,920
The reason why I want to focus on this key constant intensity is that as we look at

157
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:40,960
different parts of the system, we're going to look at the intensity of the different

158
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:45,040
parts of the system to kind of get a whole picture of where greenhouse gases are coming

159
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:49,200
from, just like we're going to look at energy intensity, in terms of how much energy do

160
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:54,400
you need for different activities, how much energy do different regions of the world use.

161
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:59,920
And so to give you a few examples of the components of the parties, the COP meetings that the

162
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:07,480
UN framework invention on climate change runs every year, they have reporting inventory.

163
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:12,680
And when every country, which is party to the Paris Agreement, has to report its greenhouse

164
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:16,480
gas emissions, and frankly, the actions that they're taking to reduce their greenhouse

165
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:21,640
gases, they have to measure or report their greenhouse gases in terms of a specific inventory

166
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:22,640
format.

167
00:12:22,640 --> 00:12:24,120
So I've linked to that here.

168
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:29,880
You can click on it and you can see how countries report different kinds of greenhouse gases.

169
00:12:29,880 --> 00:12:36,160
It's because countries can only see the source of the activities that create greenhouse gases.

170
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:40,320
So it's easier, of course, to measure how many barrels of oil we burned, or how many

171
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:45,240
short tons of coal we burned, or let's say how much land use conversion has led to how

172
00:12:45,240 --> 00:12:48,000
much methane or how much carbon dioxide.

173
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:54,720
We can also measure, let's say, our greenhouse gas emissions per unit of electricity, because

174
00:12:54,720 --> 00:12:56,640
electricity comes from a grid.

175
00:12:56,640 --> 00:13:00,960
The grid is a technology we'll talk about a lot in this class that mixes different sources

176
00:13:00,960 --> 00:13:01,960
of energy together.

177
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:08,240
And so we try to get an average intensity of greenhouse gas emissions per unit electricity

178
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:10,320
in different regions.

179
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:14,400
Another mission's intensity factor that people look at quite often is air quality.

180
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:19,880
We can look at how many greenhouse gases or how much particulate matter is produced

181
00:13:19,880 --> 00:13:26,280
by certain activities, like vehicle miles traveled or a number of cars in the road.

182
00:13:26,280 --> 00:13:30,280
So just to give you an example of what carbon emission intensity looks like, this is the

183
00:13:30,280 --> 00:13:36,440
carbon emission intensity of economies in 2018, and this is from our world and data.

184
00:13:36,440 --> 00:13:41,480
And you can see, actually, it's measured on the bottom legend here in terms of amount

185
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:48,480
of kilograms of carbon dioxide equivalent, the carbon dioxide intensity per dollar of

186
00:13:48,480 --> 00:13:52,640
GDP measured in international 2011 prices.

187
00:13:52,640 --> 00:13:58,240
And you can see by that measure, actually, a lot of the developed worlds, such as Australia,

188
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:04,400
Europe, North America, and Arts of Latin America, don't look very intense, if only because

189
00:14:04,400 --> 00:14:09,840
they don't produce that many greenhouse gases per dollar of a GDP output.

190
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:15,760
Now, like we talked about in the last class in terms of population, what does this hide?

191
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:20,240
Well, there's a couple of things with a map, you know, sometimes the area of the map distorts

192
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:24,840
the actual area or total emissions, you know, small places don't look like they had as much

193
00:14:24,840 --> 00:14:29,120
impact as big places, even though that really has nothing to do with the average.

194
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:33,760
But the more important point from a map like this is that it hides the fact that the rich

195
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:40,320
countries like Australia, Europe, North America, and Arts of Latin America simply have much

196
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:41,960
larger GDPs.

197
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:47,520
So it looks like the carbon dioxide output per GDP is low.

198
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:52,040
But if we look at the per capita CO2 emissions, like the point in our last class, you can

199
00:14:52,040 --> 00:14:55,920
see that for a relatively small number of people in those rich developed countries, they have

200
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:58,880
very high CO2 emissions per capita.

201
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:03,440
And so your map basically changes biometrically, and you can see the difference between richer

202
00:15:03,440 --> 00:15:05,240
countries and poorer countries.

203
00:15:05,240 --> 00:15:11,880
And overwhelmingly, the richer countries are more responsible for carbon dioxide emissions.

204
00:15:11,880 --> 00:15:17,320
If we look at this similarly in kind of a scatter plot, you can again see that, you know, it looks

205
00:15:17,320 --> 00:15:21,760
like on an emission intensity basis, India and the United States are not that different.

206
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:27,200
Of course, the differences in terms of the output of the economy on a per capita basis,

207
00:15:27,200 --> 00:15:32,720
the average person in the United States, produces much more and inevitably consumes much more

208
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:35,200
than your average person in the other Pakistan.

209
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:41,120
So even though we have equivalent emissions intensity per dollar, we simply produce and

210
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:44,040
consume much more in the richer countries.

211
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:50,680
And I make this point because after the Kyoto Protocol, and after the George W. Bush administration

212
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:55,480
chose to leave the Kyoto Protocol, one of their arguments was that the U.S. actually

213
00:15:55,480 --> 00:16:01,880
had a lower carbon emission intensity or at least a comparable carbon emission intensity

214
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:03,400
to some developing countries.

215
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:09,680
Of course, this hid the fact that the United States is both richer and consumes much more

216
00:16:09,680 --> 00:16:11,560
and produces much more.

217
00:16:11,560 --> 00:16:17,040
So that's just a way to talk about what intensities may tell us and what they may hide also.

218
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:22,080
Now, I'm going to get to a really kind of key diagram that I rather love in this class.

